@vsm 1.0
// docs/deploy/2026-08-11_ds_dispatch_boot_catalog_review.vsm
// DIRECTIVE: [O]@S3 -> [S]@S1 (DS), 2026-08-11
// Full task detail: TASKS.vsm(BOOT-PROBLEMS-CATALOG-2026-08-10).
// Catalog to review: docs/incidents/2026-08-10_boot_problems_catalog.vsm.
// Context: rmw3 asked for every boot-related problem across (what turned
// out to be) one ~6h window on 2026-08-10 — 22 items, both from the earlier
// boot audit and from BOOT-RECONCILE-2026-08-10, all independently
// re-verified before being written down (every commit hash cited resolves
// live, checked this session). Then rmw3, verbatim: "le quieres pasar esta
// lista a DS, que la complete con sus propias observaciones y veamos como
// atacamos los problemas no resueltos?"

phi([O]@S3 -> [S]@S1, directive) {
  ref:  TASKS.vsm(BOOT-PROBLEMS-CATALOG-2026-08-10)
  auth: rmw3, 2026-08-11, live session -- "le quieres pasar esta lista a DS,
      que la complete con sus propias observaciones y veamos como atacamos
      los problemas no resueltos?"

  why: "The catalog is [O]'s vantage point only -- Claude Code sessions in
      this same repo. You ran a large amount of work the same day (the
      TASK-STATUS-VOCABULARY-NORMALISE census+proposal, the NATS filesystem
      sweep referenced in item 13's remediation history, PH-09/PH-11 fuse
      publishing) that [O] did not review line by line for boot-adjacent
      findings. The catalog is explicitly marked DRAFT with an empty
      catalog_ds_addendum section for exactly this reason -- a one-sided
      retrospective is worse than an incomplete one that says so."

  task: "Two things, in the catalog file itself (docs/incidents/
      2026-08-10_boot_problems_catalog.vsm), not a separate document:
        1. Fill catalog_ds_addendum. Anything boot-adjacent from today's
           other work that a Claude-Code vantage point wouldn't see --
           NOT a full re-audit, just what you already know from having
           done the work. If there's nothing to add, say that explicitly
           rather than leaving the field silently unedited (a field that
           was reviewed-and-empty is different from one nobody looked at).
        2. For EACH of the 5 items in `unresolved[]`, add a readiness note:
           what you would execute the instant the rmw3-gate on it clears,
           so the gate becomes the ONLY remaining blocker rather than a
           design question someone has to re-derive later. Four of five are
           rmw3-only decisions you cannot make (STITCH-KEY-EXPOSURE-2026-08-10,
           GIT-HISTORY-REWRITE-2026-08-03, R7-SCOPE-INVERSION,
           `git gc --prune=now`) -- for those, readiness = 'here is exactly
           what I'd run/change, nothing more'. The fifth
           (TASK-STATUS-VOCABULARY-NORMALISE step 3) is yours to execute
           once rmw3 rules the ambiguous set -- for that one, readiness =
           confirm steps 1-2's output is still current and step 3 needs
           nothing new from you to start."

  guardrail_critico: "Do NOT execute any of the 5 unresolved items -- this
      dispatch asks for readiness notes, not action. Four are L3/A5-gated
      (hard-rule scope, credential rotation, git-history disposition --
      none reversible by a later merge); jumping ahead on any of them
      would be exactly the autonomy-ceiling violation A5 exists to prevent.
      Do not touch STITCH-KEY-EXPOSURE-2026-08-10's key material even to
      describe it more precisely -- the existing redaction convention holds.
      This is a documentation/review task -- no code changes expected, and
      none should happen as a side effect of 'while I'm in here'."

  gate: "No blocker -- start on receipt."

  result: "A commit to the catalog file with catalog_ds_addendum filled and
      all 5 unresolved[] entries carrying a readiness note. Report back
      however fits -- this one doesn't need a full session() block on its
      own if nothing else changed, a short note in the commit message plus
      an ack is enough."
}
